[4] Tournament Analysis on operator/carrier vertices
  vertices:
    q     q binding scale       -- exact LRC gap denominator
    sum   p+q additive lane     -- Stern-Brocot / n+2 recursion
    prod  p*q product lane      -- Kpq area / n*2 coimage growth
    C27   C=27 shell            -- p=2 second-gap transfer router
    K33   Kpq/K33 incidence     -- p>=3 three-owner packet
    oct   octahedron L(K4)      -- support-six current/curl
    cleb  Clebsch/half-cube     -- folded covariance/cut carrier
    PZ    Paley-Zygmund         -- second-moment existence gateway
    power power payloads        -- magnitude-leak stress tests

  conservative gauge:
    observable: theorem scale / proof-router priority
    fingerprint: score_hist=((0, 1), (1, 1), (2, 1), (3, 1), (4, 1), (5, 1), (6, 1), (7, 1), (8, 1)) c3=0 scc=(1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) hp=1
    path: q binding scale > C=27 shell > p+q additive lane > Kpq/K33 incidence > p*q product lane > octahedron L(K4) > Clebsch/half-cube > Paley-Zygmund > power payloads

  majority-of-criteria gauge:
    criteria: theorem_scale, sequence_simplicity, branch_separation, state_lift_fit, graph_packet_fit, anti_scalar_guard, magnitude_stress
    fingerprint: score_hist={0: 1, 1: 1, 3: 2, 4: 2, 6: 1, 7: 1, 8: 1} c3=2 scc=(4, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1) hp=5
    first Hamiltonian path: q binding scale > C=27 shell > Kpq/K33 incidence > p+q additive lane > p*q product lane > octahedron L(K4) > Clebsch/half-cube > Paley-Zygmund > power payloads
    directed 3-cycles: 2
      p+q additive lane > p*q product lane > Clebsch/half-cube > p+q additive lane
      p+q additive lane > octahedron L(K4) > Clebsch/half-cube > p+q additive lane
    nontrivial SCCs:
      Clebsch/half-cube, octahedron L(K4), p*q product lane, p+q additive lane

  Edge-flip lesson:
    The conservative proof order is clean, but the majority gauge creates
    a real SCC among p+q, p*q, octahedron, and Clebsch/half-cube.
    That is useful: it marks the packet layer where no single scalar
    carrier should be trusted without its side-channel labels.

[5] Proof readout
  S137 keeps the current best interface and adds one warning.
  Interface:
    exact M/Farey branch first; C=27 shell transfer for p=2; Kpq/K33
    incidence for p>=3; octahedral/Clebsch packet data if a support-six
    or folded-mask residual remains; PZ only as an existence gateway.
  Warning:
    the additive lane and product/packet lanes form a majority-cycle once
    sequence simplicity, graph fit, state-lift fit, and magnitude stress
    are all allowed to vote.  This is exactly where scalarization is risky.
  Candidate lemma shape:
    after the standard LRC14 finite-core reductions, every low-gap
    non-AP/GW atom has either a unit-visible C27 hole discharged by
    petal/two-block rigidity, or a sign-visible K33/octahedral/Clebsch
    packet whose connected OCF state-lift would have activity-two value 7.
  This is still not an LRC14 proof.  It narrows the object that a proof
  must construct before invoking the HYP-2908 / THM-572 forbidden-H end.
